\viewkind4\uc1\pard\sa160\sl276\slmult1\lang1033\f0\fs22
\pard\sa220\b\fs36 Quantiva Explorer - Interview Master Q&A\b0\fs22\par
\par
\pard\sa180\b\fs30 Project Summary\b0\fs22\par
\pard **Quantiva Explorer** is a quantum-inspired drug discovery exploration platform that helps users search, compare, score, and visualize molecular candidates using probabilistic ranking and interactive 3D interfaces.\par
\par
\pard\sa120 ------------------------------------------------------------\par
\par
\pard\sa180\b\fs30 1) What datasets are used in this project?\b0\fs22\par
\pard The project uses four core datasets:\par
\par
\pard\li360\tx360 1. **processed_dataset.json** (final merged and engineered dataset)\par
\pard\li360\tx360 2. **cleaned_dataset.csv** (tabular version of the final merged dataset)\par
\pard\li360\tx360 3. **delaney-processed.csv** (solubility-focused source dataset)\par
\pard\li360\tx360 4. **PubChem_compound_antibiotic.csv** (large PubChem-derived source dataset)\par
\par
\pard It also supports **live API retrieval** from PubChem and ChEMBL at runtime.\par
\par
\pard\sa120 ------------------------------------------------------------\par
\par
\pard\sa180\b\fs30 2) What are dataset sizes and important attributes?\b0\fs22\par
\pard\sa140\b\fs26 Dataset sizes\b0\fs22\par
\pard\li360\tx360 1. processed_dataset.json: **3,487** rows\par
\pard\li360\tx360 2. cleaned_dataset.csv: **3,487** rows\par
\pard\li360\tx360 3. delaney-processed.csv: **1,128** rows\par
\pard\li360\tx360 4. PubChem_compound_antibiotic.csv: **2,359** rows\par
\par
\pard\sa140\b\fs26 Source distribution in final merged data\b0\fs22\par
\pard\li360\tx360 1. pubchem_antibiotic: **2,359**\par
\pard\li360\tx360 2. delaney_solubility: **1,128**\par
\par
\pard\sa140\b\fs26 Important attributes in final dataset\b0\fs22\par
\pard\li360\tx360 1. candidate_id\par
\pard\li360\tx360 2. source_dataset\par
\pard\li360\tx360 3. name\par
\pard\li360\tx360 4. smiles\par
\pard\li360\tx360 5. molecular_weight\par
\pard\li360\tx360 6. polar_area\par
\pard\li360\tx360 7. xlogp\par
\pard\li360\tx360 8. h_bond_donor_count\par
\pard\li360\tx360 9. h_bond_acceptor_count\par
\pard\li360\tx360 10. rotatable_bond_count\par
\pard\li360\tx360 11. binding_score\par
\pard\li360\tx360 12. toxicity\par
\pard\li360\tx360 13. stability\par
\pard\li360\tx360 14. solubility\par
\pard\li360\tx360 15. efficacy_index\par
\pard\li360\tx360 16. safety_index\par
\pard\li360\tx360 17. molecular_complexity\par
\pard\li360\tx360 18. drug_score\par
\pard\li360\tx360 19. priority_rank\par
\par
\pard\sa120 ------------------------------------------------------------\par
\par
\pard\sa180\b\fs30 3) What are the target variables?\b0\fs22\par
\pard There is no single supervised ML target label trained inside the app runtime. Instead, the platform computes ranking targets through engineered scoring:\par
\par
\pard\li360\tx360 1. **drug_score**: primary composite ranking score\par
\pard\li360\tx360 2. **priority_rank**: final rank after sorting by drug_score\par
\par
\pard Intermediate engineered targets used for scoring:\par
\pard\li360\tx360 1. efficacy_index\par
\pard\li360\tx360 2. safety_index\par
\pard\li360\tx360 3. molecular_complexity\par
\par
\pard\sa120 ------------------------------------------------------------\par
\par
\pard\sa180\b\fs30 4) Which APIs are used in this project?\b0\fs22\par
\pard\sa140\b\fs26 External APIs\b0\fs22\par
\pard\li360\tx360 1. **PubChem PUG REST API**\par
\pard\li360\tx360 2. **ChEMBL API**\par
\pard\li360\tx360 3. **Google Gemini API** (assistant responses)\par
\par
\pard\sa140\b\fs26 Internal APIs (backend)\b0\fs22\par
\pard\li360\tx360 1. /api/meta\par
\pard\li360\tx360 2. /api/candidates\par
\pard\li360\tx360 3. /api/score\par
\pard\li360\tx360 4. /api/molecule/:name\par
\pard\li360\tx360 5. /api/disease-search\par
\pard\li360\tx360 6. /api/live-molecules\par
\pard\li360\tx360 7. /api/chat\par
\par
\pard\sa120 ------------------------------------------------------------\par
\par
\pard\sa180\b\fs30 5) What data is retrieved from APIs?\b0\fs22\par
\pard From PubChem/ChEMBL the app retrieves:\par
\pard\li360\tx360 1. Compound ID\par
\pard\li360\tx360 2. Name\par
\pard\li360\tx360 3. Molecular weight\par
\pard\li360\tx360 4. LogP\par
\pard\li360\tx360 5. H-bond donor count\par
\pard\li360\tx360 6. H-bond acceptor count\par
\pard\li360\tx360 7. SMILES\par
\pard\li360\tx360 8. Optional additional metadata (for example, TPSA, image references)\par
\par
\pard Then the backend computes normalized and derived values:\par
\pard\li360\tx360 1. efficacy_index\par
\pard\li360\tx360 2. safety_index\par
\pard\li360\tx360 3. molecular_complexity\par
\pard\li360\tx360 4. weighted_score\par
\pard\li360\tx360 5. probability\par
\par
\pard\sa120 ------------------------------------------------------------\par
\par
\pard\sa180\b\fs30 6) How many samples are retrieved from APIs?\b0\fs22\par
\pard It is configurable per endpoint.\par
\par
\pard\li360\tx360 1. Molecule-by-name endpoint: up to **50** results\par
\pard\li360\tx360 2. Disease search endpoint: up to **50** results\par
\pard\li360\tx360 3. Live molecules endpoint: default **50**, configurable up to **1000**\par
\par
\pard\sa120 ------------------------------------------------------------\par
\par
\pard\sa180\b\fs30 7) Code snippet: How API integration is implemented\b0\fs22\par
\pard\sa100\f1\fs20 
\pard // Backend live fetch strategy: PubChem -> ChEMBL -> Local fallback\par
\pard app.get('/api/live-molecules', async (req, res) => \{\par
\pard   const limit = Math.min(Math.max(Number(req.query.limit ?? 50), 1), 1000);\par
\pard   let rows = [];\par
\pard   let source = null;\par
\par
\pard   try \{\par
\pard     rows = await fetchFromPubchem(\{ limit, timeoutMs: 3000 \});\par
\pard     source = 'pubchem';\par
\pard   \} catch \{\par
\pard     try \{\par
\pard       rows = await fetchFromChembl(\{ limit, timeoutMs: 3000 \});\par
\pard       source = 'chembl';\par
\pard     \} catch \{\par
\pard       rows = await loadLocalDataset(\{ limit \});\par
\pard       source = 'local';\par
\pard     \}\par
\pard   \}\par
\par
\pard   return res.json(\{ source, count: rows.length, items: rows \});\par
\pard \});\par
\pard\sa100\f1\fs20 
\par
\pard\sa100\f1\fs20 
\pard // Frontend consumption\par
\pard const url = `$\{apiBase\}/api/live-molecules?limit=$\{limit\}&alpha=$\{alpha\}&timeoutMs=$\{timeoutMs\}`;\par
\pard const res = await fetch(url);\par
\pard\sa100\f1\fs20 
\par
\pard\sa120 ------------------------------------------------------------\par
\par
\pard\sa180\b\fs30 8) Where is "quantum" used in this project?\b0\fs22\par
\pard This is **quantum-inspired**, not quantum hardware execution.\par
\par
\pard Quantum-inspired elements:\par
\pard\li360\tx360 1. Softmax-based probabilistic ranking\par
\pard\li360\tx360 2. Probability diffusion via a quantum-walk-like process on molecular similarity graph\par
\pard\li360\tx360 3. Exploration of non-obvious candidates through probability redistribution\par
\pard\li360\tx360 4. Classical vs quantum-style comparative view\par
\par
\pard\sa120 ------------------------------------------------------------\par
\par
\pard\sa180\b\fs30 9) How are molecules ranked?\b0\fs22\par
\pard\sa140\b\fs26 Core ranking formula\b0\fs22\par
\pard Drug candidate score is a weighted combination of:\par
\pard\li360\tx360 1. efficacy\par
\pard\li360\tx360 2. safety\par
\pard\li360\tx360 3. complexity balance\par
\par
\pard Default weighted composition:\par
\pard\li360\tx360 1. efficacy: 0.45\par
\pard\li360\tx360 2. safety: 0.35\par
\pard\li360\tx360 3. complexityBalance: 0.20\par
\par
\pard Molecules are then sorted descending by score and assigned rank.\par
\par
\pard In frontend exploration mode, additional weighted scoring + softmax probabilities + optional diffusion is applied.\par
\par
\pard\sa120 ------------------------------------------------------------\par
\par
\pard\sa180\b\fs30 10) Important points and concepts to remember\b0\fs22\par
\pard\li360\tx360 1. This is an **end-to-end platform**: data ingestion, harmonization, feature engineering, ranking, visualization, and reporting.\par
\pard\li360\tx360 2. Uses **multi-source molecular data** with schema harmonization.\par
\pard\li360\tx360 3. Includes **resilient fallback architecture** (PubChem -> ChEMBL -> Local).\par
\pard\li360\tx360 4. Supports **interactive 3D molecular exploration** and comparative analysis.\par
\pard\li360\tx360 5. Implements **user authentication and persistence** for saved experiments/configurations.\par
\pard\li360\tx360 6. "Quantum" claim is **inspired algorithmic design**, not QPU execution.\par
\pard\li360\tx360 7. Strong practical focus: explainable ranking factors and reproducible experiments.\par
\par
\pard\sa120 ------------------------------------------------------------\par
\par
\pard\sa180\b\fs30 11) Resume point defense (interview-ready)\b0\fs22\par
\pard\sa140\b\fs26 Resume Point A\b0\fs22\par
\pard "Built a platform for exploring drug compounds through molecular search, AI-assisted ranking, and interactive visualization."\par
\par
\pard **How to defend:**\par
\pard\li360\tx360 1. Implemented molecular search through live API-backed endpoints and disease keyword retrieval.\par
\pard\li360\tx360 2. Built weighted scoring and probability-based ranking engine for candidate prioritization.\par
\pard\li360\tx360 3. Added interactive 2D/3D visualization and ranking dashboards for exploration.\par
\par
\pard\sa140\b\fs26 Resume Point B\b0\fs22\par
\pard "Enabled users to compare molecular properties and identify promising drug candidates using an interactive 3D visualization interface."\par
\par
\pard **How to defend:**\par
\pard\li360\tx360 1. Added side-by-side and split views (classical vs quantum-inspired scoring).\par
\pard\li360\tx360 2. Enabled property-level comparison (MW, LogP, H-bond features, scores, confidence).\par
\pard\li360\tx360 3. Used 3D molecular universe visualization to inspect clusters and high-value candidates interactively.\par
\par
\pard\sa120 ------------------------------------------------------------\par
\par
\pard\sa180\b\fs30 12) Project architecture (for interview whiteboard drawing)\b0\fs22\par
\pard Use this simple 6-block architecture in interview:\par
\par
\pard\li360\tx360 1. **User Interface Layer (React + TypeScript + Three.js)**\par
\pard    - Search, filters, ranking dashboard, 3D visualization, assistant UI\par
\par
\pard\li360\tx360 2. **Application Logic Layer (Frontend State + Scoring Controller)**\par
\pard    - Weight tuning, simulation controls, diffusion toggles, result orchestration\par
\par
\pard\li360\tx360 3. **API Gateway Layer (Node.js + Express)**\par
\pard    - Unified REST endpoints for candidates, scoring, molecule search, disease search, chat\par
\par
\pard\li360\tx360 4. **Data Integration Layer**\par
\pard    - PubChem connector\par
\pard    - ChEMBL connector\par
\pard    - Local dataset fallback loader\par
\par
\pard\li360\tx360 5. **Scoring & Intelligence Layer**\par
\pard    - Feature engineering\par
\pard    - Weighted ranking\par
\pard    - Softmax probability assignment\par
\pard    - Quantum-inspired probability diffusion\par
\par
\pard\li360\tx360 6. **Persistence Layer (MongoDB + JSON fallback)**\par
\pard    - Users, experiments, configs, history, reports\par
\par
\pard Suggested interview drawing flow:\par
\pard **UI -> API -> Integrations/Data -> Scoring Engine -> Persistence -> UI**\par
\par
\pard\sa120 ------------------------------------------------------------\par
\par
\pard\sa180\b\fs30 13) Technologies used\b0\fs22\par
\pard\li360\tx360 1. React.js\par
\pard\li360\tx360 2. TypeScript\par
\pard\li360\tx360 3. Three.js / React Three Fiber / Drei\par
\pard\li360\tx360 4. Node.js\par
\pard\li360\tx360 5. Express.js\par
\pard\li360\tx360 6. MongoDB + Mongoose\par
\pard\li360\tx360 7. JWT authentication\par
\pard\li360\tx360 8. bcrypt password hashing\par
\pard\li360\tx360 9. PubChem and ChEMBL APIs\par
\pard\li360\tx360 10. Gemini API integration (assistant mode)\par
\par
\pard\sa120 ------------------------------------------------------------\par
\par
\pard\sa180\b\fs30 14) Challenges faced and how they were solved\b0\fs22\par
\pard\li360\tx360 1. **Heterogeneous dataset schemas**\par
\pard    - Solved via schema harmonization + feature engineering pipeline.\par
\pard\li360\tx360 2. **External API reliability and latency**\par
\pard    - Solved with timeout handling, caching, and fallback hierarchy.\par
\pard\li360\tx360 3. **Interpretable ranking**\par
\pard    - Solved with explicit weighted scoring and visible metrics.\par
\pard\li360\tx360 4. **Visualization performance for many molecules**\par
\pard    - Solved using efficient rendering approaches and sampling controls.\par
\pard\li360\tx360 5. **User-specific experiment persistence**\par
\pard    - Solved with auth + user-scoped storage models.\par
\par
\pard\sa120 ------------------------------------------------------------\par
\par
\pard\sa180\b\fs30 15) Does this solution already exist? If yes, what is your novelty?\b0\fs22\par
\pard\sa140\b\fs26 Existing landscape\b0\fs22\par
\pard Yes, molecular viewers, cheminformatics tools, and docking/ranking systems exist (for example, PubChem browser tools, ChEMBL browsing, and specialized drug-design software).\par
\par
\pard\sa140\b\fs26 Your novelty\b0\fs22\par
\pard\li360\tx360 1. **Unified educational + exploratory workflow** in one platform (search + rank + compare + 3D visualize + save experiments).\par
\pard\li360\tx360 2. **Quantum-inspired probabilistic exploration layer** integrated with practical web UX.\par
\pard\li360\tx360 3. **Hybrid reliability strategy** (live API first, local fallback always available).\par
\pard\li360\tx360 4. **Interview/demo-friendly explainability** (clear score components and ranking logic).\par
\pard\li360\tx360 5. **Fast prototyping architecture** suitable for hackathon and extensible for research tooling.\par
\par
\pard\sa120 ------------------------------------------------------------\par
\par
\pard\sa180\b\fs30 16) 60-second closing pitch (memorize)\b0\fs22\par
\pard "Quantiva Explorer is a quantum-inspired molecular exploration platform that integrates public chemical data, engineered drug-likeness scoring, and interactive 3D visualization to help researchers quickly identify promising candidates. It combines weighted ranking, probabilistic diffusion, and multi-source data fallback for robust, explainable, and scalable exploration. Beyond visualization, it supports reproducible experiments with user-scoped persistence, making it both practical for demos and extensible for serious drug discovery workflows."\par
\par
\pard\sa120 ------------------------------------------------------------\par
\par
\pard\sa180\b\fs30 17) Additional Interview FAQs (ML and AI clarity)\b0\fs22\par
\pard\sa140\b\fs26 Q1) What ML models are used?\b0\fs22\par
\pard **Answer:**\par
\pard The current implementation is primarily a **rule-based and probabilistic ranking system**, not a deep supervised ML pipeline with a trained classifier/regressor checkpoint.\par
\par
\pard What is used:\par
\pard\li360\tx360 1. Engineered feature scoring (efficacy, safety, complexity)\par
\pard\li360\tx360 2. Weighted composite ranking\par
\pard\li360\tx360 3. Softmax probability normalization\par
\pard\li360\tx360 4. Quantum-inspired probability diffusion over similarity graph\par
\par
\pard AI/LLM used:\par
\pard\li360\tx360 1. Gemini integration is used for assistant-style explanations and conversational help.\par
\par
\pard Interview-safe line:\par
\pard "We use AI-assisted ranking and explanation layers with probabilistic scoring and diffusion, but this version does not train a custom end-to-end deep model in production."\par
\par
\pard\sa140\b\fs26 Q2) Why is it called AI-assisted?\b0\fs22\par
\pard **Answer:**\par
\pard It is called AI-assisted because the platform uses:\par
\pard\li360\tx360 1. AI-style scoring heuristics for candidate prioritization\par
\pard\li360\tx360 2. Probabilistic ranking and diffusion to surface non-obvious candidates\par
\pard\li360\tx360 3. LLM-powered assistant for contextual molecule/project guidance\par
\par
\pard So the system **assists decision-making** for researchers rather than claiming autonomous clinical decision output.\par
\par
\pard\sa140\b\fs26 Q3) Do we have classification based on disease?\b0\fs22\par
\pard **Answer:**\par
\pard Not as a trained disease classifier model. Instead, the platform has **disease-aware profile logic**:\par
\pard\li360\tx360 1. Disease keyword/category inference\par
\pard\li360\tx360 2. Category-specific weight adjustments\par
\pard\li360\tx360 3. Disease-targeted retrieval via disease keyword search\par
\par
\pard This is profile-driven prioritization, not a medical diagnosis classifier.\par
\par
\pard\sa140\b\fs26 Q4) How many diseases/categories are listed?\b0\fs22\par
\pard **Answer:**\par
\pard The app currently supports **6 therapeutic categories**:\par
\pard\li360\tx360 1. Cancer\par
\pard\li360\tx360 2. Infectious\par
\pard\li360\tx360 3. CNS / Neurological\par
\pard\li360\tx360 4. Metabolic\par
\pard\li360\tx360 5. Cardiovascular\par
\pard\li360\tx360 6. General\par
\par
\pard\sa140\b\fs26 Q5) Does it provide a single drug or a combination of drugs?\b0\fs22\par
\pard **Answer:**\par
\pard It currently returns a **ranked list of individual candidate molecules**.\par
\pard It does **not** generate optimized multi-drug combination therapy plans in the current implementation.\par
\par
\pard\sa140\b\fs26 Q6) Is Lipinski used here? What is it?\b0\fs22\par
\pard **Answer:**\par
\pard Yes, Lipinski is used in both dataset engineering and frontend scoring checks.\par
\par
\pard **Lipinski Rule of Five** is a classic drug-likeness heuristic that checks if a compound is likely to have good oral bioavailability:\par
\pard\li360\tx360 1. Molecular weight <= 500\par
\pard\li360\tx360 2. LogP <= 5\par
\pard\li360\tx360 3. H-bond donors <= 5\par
\pard\li360\tx360 4. H-bond acceptors <= 10\par
\par
\pard How it is used in this project:\par
\pard\li360\tx360 1. Lipinski compliance contributes to safety/drug-likeness evaluation.\par
\pard\li360\tx360 2. It affects filtering and ranking confidence.\par
\pard\li360\tx360 3. It is exposed in UI explanations for interpretability.\par
\par
\pard\sa120 ------------------------------------------------------------\par
\par
\pard\sa180\b\fs30 18) Sharp one-liners for interviewer cross-questions\b0\fs22\par
\pard\li360\tx360 1. **Model question:** "This version focuses on engineered probabilistic intelligence rather than training a monolithic black-box model."\par
\pard\li360\tx360 2. **AI claim question:** "AI-assisted here means ranked decision support plus LLM explanations, not autonomous prescription."\par
\pard\li360\tx360 3. **Disease question:** "We support disease-aware prioritization profiles, not diagnostic disease classification."\par
\pard\li360\tx360 4. **Output question:** "Current output is prioritized single compounds; combination therapy is future scope."\par
\pard\li360\tx360 5. **Lipinski question:** "Lipinski is one of our core drug-likeness constraints for safe, explainable prioritization."\par
}

